      {
        "id": "stage-12-topic-8-section-16",
        "title": "PromQL Fundamentals — Observability and Monitoring Fundamentals — PromQL Fundamentals [stage-12-topic-8-section-16]",
        "content": "# PromQL Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Technical scope and purpose\n\nThis lesson covers PromQL Fundamentals within PromQL Fundamentals in the stage-12 pipeline. Its technical focus is PromQL, Fundamentals. The purpose is to understand the component's responsibilities, its lifecycle, the dependencies it relies on and the operational conditions under which it succeeds or fails.\n\n## 2. Architecture and operational dependencies\n\nThe relevant engineering area includes component responsibilities, execution lifecycle, configuration boundaries, operational dependencies and production verification. Identify the control-plane components, execution components, configuration sources, external services and downstream consumers. Establish which component owns the desired state, which component performs the operation and which component reports the resulting state.\n\n## 3. Implementation and execution\n\nIdentify the configuration files, runtime components, upstream dependencies and downstream consumers involved. Validate the intended configuration before changing production state. Execute the smallest controlled change and retain command output, logs and configuration evidence.\n\nDocument the exact inputs, configuration values, permissions, versions and execution context. Separate preparation, execution and post-execution verification. Do not promote an unverified configuration or assume that a successful command guarantees application health.\n\n## 4. Validation and production evidence\n\nVerify the expected state through the relevant CLI, API, controller status, logs and application behavior. Compare actual results with explicit acceptance criteria and preserve evidence for review.\n\nRetain the relevant command output, resource status, timestamps, logs, revision identifiers and change references. Compare the observed state with the intended state. Record both successful checks and failed acceptance criteria.\n\n## 5. Failure analysis and troubleshooting\n\nInvestigate failures by separating configuration errors, permission problems, dependency failures, resource exhaustion, connectivity problems and runtime defects. Start with the earliest failed operation. Correlate timestamps across the pipeline and affected services. Change one suspected cause at a time and verify whether the observed symptom changes.\n\n## 6. Recovery and operational safeguards\n\nUse a known-good configuration or revision when recovery is required. Confirm the impact and dependencies before repeating an operation. Protect production data, preserve diagnostic evidence and verify the recovered state before restoring normal traffic or continuing downstream pipeline execution.\n\n## 7. Production acceptance criteria\n\nAccept the implementation only when the intended behavior is demonstrated, operational dependencies are healthy, security boundaries remain intact, monitoring provides sufficient evidence and the recovery procedure is understood by the responsible team.\n\n## 8. Section traceability\n\nStage: stage-12\nNavigation: PromQL Fundamentals\nSection: stage-12-topic-8-section-16\nSubject: PromQL Fundamentals"
      },
