        "title": "Production Hands-on Lab — Observability and Monitoring Fundamentals — Production Hands-on Lab [stage-12-topic-20-section-16]",
        "content": "# Production Hands-on Lab\n\n## 1. Technical scope and purpose\n\nThis lesson covers Production Hands-on Lab within Production Hands-on Lab in the stage-12 pipeline. Its technical focus is Production, Hands, Lab. The purpose is to understand the component's responsibilities, its lifecycle, the dependencies it relies on and the operational conditions under which it succeeds or fails.\n\n## 2. Architecture and operational dependencies\n\nThe relevant engineering area includes component responsibilities, execution lifecycle, configuration boundaries, operational dependencies and production verification. Identify the control-plane components, execution components, configuration sources, external services and downstream consumers. Establish which component owns the desired state, which component performs the operation and which component reports the resulting state.\n\n## 3. Implementation and execution\n\nIdentify the configuration files, runtime components, upstream dependencies and downstream consumers involved. Validate the intended configuration before changing production state. Execute the smallest controlled change and retain command output, logs and configuration evidence.\n\nDocument the exact inputs, configuration values, permissions, versions and execution context. Separate preparation, execution and post-execution verification. Do not promote an unverified configuration or assume that a successful command guarantees application health.\n\n## 4. Validation and production evidence\n\nVerify the expected state through the relevant CLI, API, controller status, logs and application behavior. Compare actual results with explicit acceptance criteria and preserve evidence for review.\n\nRetain the relevant command output, resource status, timestamps, logs, revision identifiers and change references. Compare the observed state with the intended state. Record both successful checks and failed acceptance criteria.\n\n## 5. Failure analysis and troubleshooting\n\nInvestigate failures by separating configuration errors, permission problems, dependency failures, resource exhaustion, connectivity problems and runtime defects. Start with the earliest failed operation. Correlate timestamps across the pipeline and affected services. Change one suspected cause at a time and verify whether the observed symptom changes.\n\n## 6. Recovery and operational safeguards\n\nUse a known-good configuration or revision when recovery is required. Confirm the impact and dependencies before repeating an operation. Protect production data, preserve diagnostic evidence and verify the recovered state before restoring normal traffic or continuing downstream pipeline execution.\n\n## 7. Production acceptance criteria\n\nAccept the implementation only when the intended behavior is demonstrated, operational dependencies are healthy, security boundaries remain intact, monitoring provides sufficient evidence and the recovery procedure is understood by the responsible team.\n\n## 8. Section traceability\n\nStage: stage-12\nNavigation: Production Hands-on Lab\nSection: stage-12-topic-20-section-16\nSubject: Production Hands-on Lab"
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-12-topic-20-section-17",
        "title": "Production Hands-on Lab — Metrics Architecture and Collection — Production Hands-on Lab [stage-12-topic-20-section-17]",
        "content": "# Production Hands-on Lab — Metrics Architecture and Collection\n\n## Concept and technical explanation\n\nThis section examines Metrics Architecture and Collection as it applies specifically to Production Hands-on Lab. Understand\nthe underlying technology, the components involved, the inputs and outputs, and the\nconditions under which this feature is used in a real DevOps delivery environment.\n\n## Architecture and execution\n\nTrace the relevant component interactions. Identify the initiating component,\nprocessing component, configuration inputs, permissions, dependencies, resulting\nstate and observable outputs. Explain how the feature connects to the surrounding\npipeline stages.\n\n## Implementation\n\nImplement this concept using the actual technology and configuration relevant to\nProduction Hands-on Lab. Use the appropriate source files, commands, YAML, JSON, scripts or platform\nconfiguration. Explain each important setting and why it is required.\n\n## Practical verification\n\nExecute the implementation in a controlled environment. Verify the expected result\nusing command output, logs, API responses, generated artifacts, platform status or\nruntime behaviour. Distinguish a successful command from a genuinely healthy result.\n\n## Failure analysis and troubleshooting\n\nInvestigate realistic failures associated with Production Hands-on Lab and Metrics Architecture and Collection. Collect\nevidence, identify the responsible component, explain the root cause, apply a targeted\ncorrection and repeat verification. Avoid blind retries and destructive recovery.\n\n## Production application\n\nExplain how this capability is used in a production DevOps workflow. Cover security,\nreliability, maintainability, performance, operational ownership and its effect on\ndownstream pipeline stages.\r\n\r\n## Section-specific lesson identity\n\nStage file: stage-12.json\nNavigation topic: Production Hands-on Lab\nSection ID: stage-12-topic-20-section-17\nLesson title: Production Hands-on Lab — Metrics Architecture and Collection — Production Hands-on Lab [stage-12-topic-20-section-17]\n\nUse this section ID and navigation topic when validating implementation,\ncommands, configuration examples, operational evidence, failure conditions,\nrecovery procedures, and production acceptance criteria for this lesson."
